From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DjDeveloperr <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 19:07:36 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: apple: context-switch the AMX matrix coprocessor

Apple CPUs that set AIDR_EL1 bit 4 have AMX, a matrix coprocessor that
EL0 drives with instructions in the reserved 0x00201000 encoding space
(Accelerate on macOS). Linux leaves it disabled. Measured on the M2 Pro
(t6020) with the kernel at EL2:

  - With AMX_CTL.EN (bit 63 of s3_4_c15_c1_4, the EL2 copy under VHE)
    clear, every AMX instruction from EL0 traps with ESR 0xfe000003
    (IMPLEMENTATION DEFINED EC 0x3f, ISS 3). m1n1 leaves it clear.
  - AMX SET makes the 5 KiB register file (8 X, 8 Y, 64 Z rows of 64
    bytes) usable; CLR discards it. AMX_STATE_T (s3_4_c15_c1_3) bit 0
    says whether this CPU is between the two, and AMX_CTL bit 8 makes a
    SET while already SET fault (ISS 2).
  - The state belongs to the CPU: a thread that moves CPU between SET
    and CLR faults on the new one, and the old one stays SET. With EN
    simply forced on, a process that exited while SET left its CPU SET
    and the Mac hard-reset within a minute.
  - The kernel can save and reload the register file with the same
    load/store instructions, as long as the operand's address field
    (bits 55:0) gets the kernel address without its top byte.

Give threads AMX the way macOS does. It stays disabled until a thread
issues its first AMX instruction; bad_el0_sync() then recognises the
EC 0x3f/ISS 3 trap, allocates the thread's save area, enables AMX and
retries the instruction. On every switch away from such a thread a SET
register file is saved and the CPU is CLRed, and switching back SETs
and reloads it, so no CPU ever runs another thread or idles with a
thread's AMX state SET. fork and clone copy the state, execve drops it,
and the save area is freed with the task.

Signal delivery saves a SET register file in an APPLE_AMX_MAGIC frame
record and CLRs the CPU, so a handler starts clear and can use AMX
itself; sigreturn reloads the record (which the handler may edit) and
leaves the thread SET again. A handler that leaves through siglongjmp
leaves AMX clear, as ZA is for SME. ptrace and core dumps get the state
as the NT_AURORA_APPLE_AMX regset (struct user_apple_amx).

Only CPUs without SME use it: M1 to M3 have AMX, while M4 and later
(and the A18 Pro) implement the architectural SME instead.

PR_AURORA_GET_AMX reports whether AMX is available and whether the
calling thread uses it, and apple_amx=off keeps AMX disabled. Below EL2
AMX_CTL_EL1 and AMX_STATE_T trap to the hypervisor, and AMX only works at
EL1/EL0 if EL2 sets AMX_CTL_EL2.EN_EL1 (bit 62), so there the capability
needs the hypervisor to say it provides AMX (Aurora vendor hypervisor
feature ARM_SMCCC_KVM_FUNC_AURORA_APPLE_AMX, 93).

Userspace learns that the feature is available from HWCAP3_APPLE_AMX
(bit 60 of AT_HWCAP3; Aurora's Apple features are allocated from the
top down, clear of the architectural bits), from "apple_amx" in the
Features line of /proc/cpuinfo, or from PR_AURORA_GET_AMX, which fails
with EINVAL when it is not.

Signed-off-by: DjDeveloperr <hey@djdev.me>
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
(cherry picked from commit 73f905f8bca9ae28421b4d02221162ffc2b7d38b)
